  "notes": "Bankhead's chant-hook lane, and the one entry here whose colour is authored as a *scale* rather than as a chord. The research: \"minor with a strong raised-4th/blues-3rd colouring in the lead line; single-mode, no modulation\" — the blues scale is the only thing in the vocabulary that carries both of those notes, so it leads the weights and `natural_minor` trails. \"Single bright saw/square lead as the entire hook, 2-bar loop, static i or i–VII; almost no pad layer\" is why `melody.loopVerbatim` is true and `maxChords` is 2: the lead *is* the harmonic content, so `chords.impliedByRiff` is set and the chord part's variety comes from `chordDurationBeats` and three `harmonicRhythm` values rather than from progressions the record does not contain. The doubled clap the research describes — \"clap on 3 with a doubled clap on the 4th 16th before it\" — is `snare.ghost` at `2a` with an unusually high probability (0.55), which is what makes this beat legible against every other clap-on-3 entry in the batch. ⚠ **[LOW-CONF] on the kick.** The research marks kick placement specifics low-confidence explicitly; `secondaryAnchor` `3&` is that claim, and `syncopation` 0.3 is a guess at how much else the pattern does. ⚠ `confidence` is `medium` overall: chart and era facts are solid, production detail is inferred from the released catalog. INFERRED: every velocity figure, the `slideProb`/`slidesPer4Bars` pair standing in for \"a rapid double-hit before the downbeat\" (the engine has no repeated-note device, so a slide into the bar line is the nearest honest thing), and the whole `intervals`/`contour` split. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
